{"name":"Affinity","hex":"#fed2a5","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"affinity","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A natural attraction or feeling of kinship to a person or thing.","A family relationship through marriage of a relative (e.g. sister-in-law), as opposed to consanguinity (e.g. sister)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/affinity"}],"entries":[{"title":"Affinity (law)","blocks":[{"text":"In law and in cultural anthropology, affinity is the kinship relationship created or that exists between two people as a result of someone's marriage. It is the relationship each party in the marriage has to the family of the other party in the marriage. It does not cover the marital relationship itself."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affinity_(law)","wikidata":"Q954007"},{"title":"Affinity analysis","blocks":[{"text":"Affinity analysis falls under the umbrella term of data mining which uncovers meaningful correlations between different entities according to their co-occurrence in a data set. In almost all systems and processes, the application of affinity analysis can extract significant knowledge about the unexpected trends. In fact, affinity analysis takes advantages of studying attributes that go together which helps uncover the hidden patterns in a big data through generating association rules. Association rules mining procedure is two-fold: first, it finds all frequent attributes in a data set and, then generates association rules satisfying some predefined criteria, support and confidence, to identify the most important relationships in the frequent itemset. The first step in the process is to count the co-occurrence of attributes in the data set. Next, a subset is created called the frequent itemset. The association rules mining takes the form of if a condition or feature (A) is present then another condition or feature (B) exists. The first condition or feature (A) is called antecedent and the latter (B) is known as consequent. This process is repeated until no additional frequent itemsets are found. There are two important metrics for performing the association rules mining technique: support and confidence. Also, a priori algorithm is used to reduce the search space for the problem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/AffinityAnalysis.png/330px-AffinityAnalysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affinity_analysis","wikidata":"Q727515"},{"title":"Affinity (software)","blocks":[{"text":"Affinity is a graphics editor developed by Serif, a subsidiary of Canva. It is simultaneously a vector graphics editor, a raster graphics editor and a page layout application."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/Affinity_3.0.0.png/330px-Affinity_3.0.0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affinity_(software)","wikidata":"Q136679398"},{"title":"Affinity (medieval)","blocks":[{"text":"In post-classical history, an affinity was a group (retinue) of (usually) men whom a lord gathered around himself in his service; it has been described by one modern historian as \"the servants, retainers, and other followers of a lord\", and as \"part of the normal fabric of society\". It is considered a fundamental aspect of bastard feudalism, and acted as a means of tying magnates to the lower nobility, just as feudalism had done in a different way."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg/330px-British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affinity_(medieval)","wikidata":"Q30603125"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/AffinityAnalysis.png/500px-AffinityAnalysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/AffinityAnalysis.png/500px-AffinityAnalysis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frequent Itemsets","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAffinityAnalysis.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/Affinity_3.0.0.png/330px-Affinity_3.0.0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/Affinity_3.0.0.png/330px-Affinity_3.0.0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAffinity_3.0.0.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg/500px-British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg/500px-British_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Dunstable Swan Jewel, a livery badge, from ca. 1400 (British Museum) ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABritish_Museum_-Dunstable_Swan_Jewel_-side_cropped_close.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Bayeux_Tapestry_scene23_Harold_sacramentum_fecit_Willelmo_duci.jpg/500px-Bayeux_Tapestry_scene23_Harold_sacramentum_fecit_Willelmo_duci.jpg?utm_source